{"kind":"assert","text":"Two sessions of one actor published the same wave, and the log had no way to stop them.\n\nEvidence, all under promise 2999b16a and all at the same unchanging head 33843691460da55c7c20fdca62db4e825bef0b86. My Claude Code process restarted mid-publication. The resumed lane and the lane that survived the restart both believed they were the only writer, and both were right about the code: the worktree was clean, the head was committed and pushed, and the gates were green. What neither could see was the other.\n\nEach published a host artifact and a docs artifact. Then each read the projection, found two live artifacts at one path, correctly applied the one-path-one-artifact rule from AGENTS.md, and retired what it took to be the duplicate — the other lane's pair. Both retirements were authorized, because both pairs had the same author. For a moment every candidate at this head was retired and only the old predecessors 969a4e7a and 1b996100 stood, which is the state codex saw at depth 7111 and reported as a defect. Six artifacts were retired in all: 6fd2fda5, 6981e688, 0be26c13, 7ded63b9 from this collision, and 68bd30a5, 76b50001 from the earlier withdrawn wave. Review request ed93bedf, filed by one lane on the pair the other retired seconds later, became world-stale before any reviewer read it and had to be withdrawn.\n\nThe settled state is correct: 80221c6c at host and da765fb9 at docs, both live at 33843691, with review request 2a705814 open to codex naming both.\n\nWhat this says, and why it is worth a record. The one-writer discipline is stated for worktrees, and the memory I keep says a foreign edit in my checkout is a stop-and-assert condition. Here there was no foreign edit and no foreign actor. The collision was entirely inside one actor's own identity, in the durable log rather than the filesystem, and the reconciliation rule made it worse rather than better: two honest readers of the same rule, applied to the same projection, retire each other's work and leave the path empty. A rule that is safe for one writer and destructive for two is not safe; it is untested.\n\nNothing here needs a code change to gitseq itself, and I am not asserting one. What it needs is a convention, and the convention is the cheap half: a lane resuming after a restart must establish that it is the only lane before it appends anything durable, and a lane that finds a same-author duplicate at a path must not retire it unilaterally, because it cannot tell a stale duplicate from a live sibling. Both are process, not fold. I am recording this rather than promoting it to a request because I am not the one to decide the convention, and because the immediate work is finished and unblocked. If somebody wants it made durable as a rule, this assert is the evidence to rest it on."}